import SiteLayout from '../../components/SiteLayout.astro';
---
<SiteLayout title="Trust and transparency — Ember" description="How Ember currently handles setup, wallet information, XMRig provisioning and process lifecycle, and what remains unverified.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-intro trust-intro"><span class="page-kicker">EMBER / TRUST & TRANSPARENCY</span><h1>Say what happens. Be clear about what hasn't been proven.</h1><p>Mining software deserves precise boundaries. This page separates current implementation from open release work; it is not a security certification.</p></header>
    <section class="trust-principle" aria-label="Ember trust principle"><blockquote>Trust comes from what you can inspect and <em>control.</em></blockquote><p>Ember's current design is local-first and consent-led. Those implementation choices reduce some risks, but they do not make local software tamper-proof or replace independent security review.</p></section>
    <section class="trust-section" aria-labelledby="implemented-title"><div class="trust-section-header"><h2 id="implemented-title">Implemented foundations</h2><span>IN THE CURRENT REPOSITORY</span></div><div class="trust-ledger">
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">01</span><div><h3>Local setup, no Ember account</h3><p>Core setup and session coordination run on the Windows machine. An explicit setup review and start action are required; app launch does not start mining.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">02</span><div><h3>Receiving address, not wallet custody</h3><p>Setup accepts a Monero mainnet public receiving address. Ember is not a wallet and does not request seed phrases or private keys; the saved address is masked in the UI.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">03</span><div><h3>Pinned XMRig provisioning</h3><p>The current Windows x64 path uses XMRig 6.26.0 from the official release. It verifies the detached signature on the checksum manifest against a pinned signer, checks the archive digest, and rechecks the installed executable digest.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">04</span><div><h3>Supervised process tree</h3><p>Rust starts the executable directly without a shell, assigns the suspended Windows process to a kill-on-close Job Object, then resumes it. Stop or Ember shutdown cleans up the process tree Ember owns.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">05</span><div><h3>Bounded local status</h3><p>The internal XMRig API is loopback-only and authenticated with a Rust-held per-run token. Tokens and session configuration do not cross into React or appear in copied diagnostics.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">06</span><div><h3>Security tools stay in control</h3><p>Ember does not disable Defender or SmartScreen, create exclusions, elevate to bypass a block, or restore quarantined files. The owner can review a security alert and retry or use verified Repair.</p></div></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="trust-section" aria-labelledby="open-work-title"><div class="trust-section-header"><h2 id="open-work-title">Open work and limits</h2><span>NOT A RELEASE CLAIM</span></div><div class="trust-ledger">
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">A</span><div><h3>Production session verification</h3><p>Owner-machine Start → Mining → Stop remains unresolved after XMRig failed to open the expected loopback API. A local “Ready” state is not evidence that pool mining succeeded.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">B</span><div><h3>Distribution and clean-machine checks</h3><p>Public release is not ready. Legal/distribution review and controlled Defender/SmartScreen testing on clean machines remain open.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">C</span><div><h3>Ember Contribution is inactive</h3><p>The documented 5% figure is a product-direction baseline only. No Ember contribution mechanism, destination or accounting exists. XMRig's upstream donation is a separate disclosed setting.</p></div></article>
      <article class="trust-entry"><span class="trust-entry-code">D</span><div><h3>Local verification has limits</h3><p>Verification detects missing or changed files within its model. It does not defend against a malicious actor with access to the same user account or prove the whole application is secure.</p></div></article>
    </div><div class="trust-scope-note"><strong>What Ember does not do:</strong> hold private keys, run hidden mining, provide an Ember-owned pool, promise profitability, submit telemetry to a server or offer a public download today.</div><a class="repo-link" href="https://github.com/uxillary/tray-xmr/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md" rel="noreferrer">Review the repository security model <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
